 it  in a sense connects to a broader human  impulse to deify people you know I think  you look at the Saints there's  people elevated to the status of gods  essentially with the same and there's  the same physical fetishism you know  finger bones the old churches claim to  have the finger bones of a particular  saint or so I think it's always it's  it's always been there but definitely I  think now the rate at which we consume  media produces celebrities very quickly  and increasingly for no reason I mean  people there are people whose jobs or  just to be famous kind of innocence and  I think that industry is getting more  and more insular and more unrelated to  to anything else it's interesting this  obviously in the film it takes it to an  asura extreme doesn't it you know you're  injecting people are getting injected  with celebrities coals and various  infections and things like you said  yourself and those you know people  eating celebrities sell steak so steaks  made out of the cells and celebrities so  obviously a little bit viewer andre royo  now i think what's the worst real life  example you think you've come across of  that excessive celebrity culture not I'm  not sure what the worst is really while  some people were reporting that my  sister dated Rob Pattinson which I  thought was really weird there's  actually a magazine I saw it in  Australia that liked photoshopped them  together and as though they were  attending a party together and there was  this weird so like I don't know if  that's the worst example but it was  weird because it was so obviously not  true and not related to anything but  it's just like you know if there's a  story there it's you know no one has it  dates to create something totally  fictional just to cause controversy it's  a little bit about the look in the style  of film as well cuz it's very  distinctive isn't it tell me about where  you got the idea to kind of to film it  in that particular kind of clean white  washed looking way well there's sort of  - I mean there's the very sterile  aspects of the film and the production  design and in the shooting and then  there the more dirty it's grimy elements  the butcher shop and the you know the  macroscopic bodily stuff so I guess it  was just  well part of the the whiteness of it I  guess was an interesting way to control  the eye of the viewer so if you have a  completely white frame in the clinics  and then you have these the celebrity  faces on the walls it lends them a  certain weight a visual weight or you  know blood pops against a white  background and so there's a blood theme  throughout the film and so the blood  ends up having the significance but also  it's that again the the divide between  the icon the inhuman the sort of  theoretical people the celebrities as  those characters and then the real  animal body that's decaying and living  and dying beneath that I wanted to  represent that in the in the design and  in the look so those two elements are  represented and then kind of collide  towards then incredible young act in the  lead role haven't you tell me about how  you found him well I mean I had I had  seen him in ax men he was in x-men first  class and I was also the kid on a bike  at the end of no country for old men but  I hadn't been thinking of him until we  started looking for actors and then I  saw a reel of work that he had done and  we all got very excited about him  because it's you know a great actor and  has that special interesting thing that  some actors have that that fascinating  screen quality and he was very nuanced  physical actor and we needed that so so  we got excited about him and then he was  reading the script and he flew to  Toronto to talk with us about it and it  came together very quickly at that point  and tell me a little bit as well about  the inspirations that you've had for  your particular style of filmmaking so  as we say your first feature this one so  who's been really having an influence on  you so far I'm not sure exactly to be  honest I know I've been influenced by a  lot of people but it's all sort of  indirect because I wasn't specifically  trying to emulate anyone  we all drink pre-production got very  excited about the film dogtooth  and and Alps is next film but I don't  think those influences really were too  visible in the final film I wanted to  ask us well where you thought the  celebrity is session that we see in  anti-viral might end eventually it's  hard to say where this culture is going  maybe will destroy civilization and then  you know people will still be famous for  various things I think I don't know if  it's inherent to to who we are I don't  think much is inherent to who we are but  I think again it's it's tied to that  religious impulse I think the desire to  kind of elevate people and to tear them  apart is something that we have  generally as a species so I don't know  what's in store for us but I imagine  will be more of that in some form and  the idea of the celebrity cell stakes as  well well how did that come to you cuz  it's so kind of gruesome in a way isn't  it right well it's based on real  technology I mean they are developing  cell cell stakes not human cell stakes  but cows all stakes and based on muscle  cells they're cultivated and so we will  probably see grown meat on the market  and within the next decade it's very  close to being I mean it exists but it's  just still too disgusting to really  market to people so it was just a small  jump to imagine human stakes that became  a good way to have them literally  consume celebrities and of course you've  got that mass outpouring of grief or  somebody who essentially nobody really  knows actually in real life in the film  don't you which we kind of see so often  in real life as well don't we is that  kind of why you took the inspiration of  that from and were you thinking of  anyone in particular yeah no I mean it's  the way some of the dialogue is lifted  a